// Fixed-width SIMD vectors (draft.md §9). `vecN<T>` (`vec2`/`vec4`/`vec8`/`vec16` of a numeric
// scalar) is an explicit SIMD register value. Build one from an array literal under a `vecN<T>`
// annotation; elementwise `+` `-` `*` `/` map to one lane-wise hardware instruction each, and
// `v[i]` reads lane `i` (a constant). This is the explicit form of the data-oriented core's
// automatic vectorization: the pipeline (`map`/`where`) never names a width, so the backend picks
// the lanes. What the pipeline *reduces* with decides whether it widens — `sum`/`min`/`max` over
// integers and `min`/`max` over floats (one `llvm.minimum`/`maximum`, NaN-propagating) do, while a
// float `sum` stays ordered because Align never reassociates floating-point silently. A
// hand-written `if x > best { best = x }` loop is a different program (an ordered comparison,
// which skips NaN), so it is not the vectorizable spelling of `max` — see `vec_argmax.align`.

fn main() -> i32 {
  a: vec4<i32> := [1, 2, 3, 4]
  b: vec4<i32> := [10, 20, 30, 40]
  // Two lane-wise instructions: c = [11, 22, 33, 44], then d = [110, 440, 990, 1760].
  c := a + b
  d := c * b
  // Read lane 2 → 990. (The process exit code is the low byte: 990 mod 256 = 222.)
  return d[2]
}
